## Current State

- Home/title screen remains intentionally unchanged from the approved version.
- `GameScene` now uses a Phaser-rendered tiled wave background and metallic board chrome.
- Combat boards scale much better across desktop, narrow desktop, and portrait mobile.
- Board title bars (`YOUR FLEET`, `ENEMY WATERS`) use the metallic chrome style aligned to the board frame.
- Ship status icons now use:
  - `src/images/Ship-Icon-01-Safe.png`
  - `src/images/Ship-Icon-02-Hit.png`
- `ALLIES` and `ENEMY` labels now use the same family as `COMBAT` and the same white-with-dark-stroke treatment.
- Enemy `MISS` tiles are transparent so the waves show through.
- Player `MISS` tiles remain visible.
- Ability buttons use the bottom-row layout and include ability-ready / ability-activated arcade burst feedback.

## Verified

- Playwright/browser checks were run directly against `GameScene`, not only by clicking through the title screen.
- Proof screenshots were generated under `test-results/board-chrome-check/`.
- Most recent verified proof for enemy-only transparent misses:
  - `test-results/board-chrome-check/enemy-only-miss-proof.png`

## Open Items

- Grid boundary transparency still needs visual tuning. The user wants:
  - boundary visible
  - transparent/translucent appearance
  - about 3px wide
  - “floating over water” effect
- The latest pass improved the effect, but the user still feels the transparent boundary is not visually correct enough.
- `ALLIES` / `ENEMY` icon styling may still be refined further after more live testing with the new assets.

## Important Constraints

- Do not change the home page unless explicitly requested.
- Keep gameplay logic unchanged while refining visuals.
- Prefer validating `GameScene` directly with Playwright by starting the scene from `window.battleshipsGame.game.scene`.
- Ignore untracked helper screenshots unless the user asks to commit them.

## Recommended Next Step

Run one focused visual pass on grid boundaries only:

1. Keep enemy-only transparent `MISS` cells as-is.
2. Tune the board fill and grid stroke together until the boundary reads as translucent instead of solid or invisible.
3. Re-verify with a direct `GameScene` Playwright screenshot before changing anything else.
